WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.500
Loops.

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.741
Python's for loop works on far more than ranges.

3
00:00:04.741 --> 00:00:07.867
Let's see how, and when to reach for while instead.

4
00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:10.433
Two loops, two jobs.

5
00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:16.767
A for loop walks any iterable: lists, dictionary keys, file lines, even live network streams.

6
00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:20.967
A while loop handles ends you can't predict, like retries and polling.

7
00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:24.067
Every loop follows the same cycle.

8
00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:25.600
Fetch the next item.

9
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.500
Check whether anything is left.

10
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.300
If so, run the body, then go around again, until the iterable is empty.

11
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.100
Idiomatic Python avoids index juggling.

12
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.733
Enumerate gives you the position, starting wherever you like.

13
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:44.233
Zip walks two lists side by side, pairing each planet with its gravity.

14
00:00:44.533 --> 00:00:47.233
But zip stops at the shorter list without any warning.

15
00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:51.267
Now a while loop that retries a flaky service.

16
00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:53.300
We count attempts, up to three.

17
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.300
If the call succeeds, break leaves the loop early.

18
00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:00.200
The else clause runs only if the loop finished without a break.

19
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.800
That means every try failed.

20
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.000
You'll see both loops in real systems.

21
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.367
DONKI is the flakiest API your agent calls.

22
00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:13.433
The retry loop we just saw is copied almost verbatim into its space-weather tool.

23
00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:20.200
And when FastAPI streams an LLM answer, the reader is just a for loop over a live network stream.

24
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:22.189
Let's recap.

25
00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:23.900
For iterates any iterable.

26
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.000
Prefer enumerate and zip to index juggling.

27
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.333
Use while when you can't predict the end.

28
00:01:29.633 --> 00:01:32.164
And remember, zip stops at the shortest.

29
00:01:32.164 --> 00:01:33.367
Next up: functions.
